// ZenTomato — build configuration.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS
// An .xcconfig is Xcode's own mechanism for build settings: a plain text file
// of KEY = VALUE lines that Xcode reads when it loads the project. It is the
// standard way an Apple project carries configuration, and it is deliberately
// the ONLY mechanism this project uses. There is no generator script and no
// intermediate format to keep in step.
//
// HOW A VALUE REACHES THE BUILD
//
//     Config/App.xcconfig  (this file, committed — safe defaults)
//        └── #include? "Secrets.xcconfig"  (git-ignored — your real values)
//              │
//              ▼
//        Xcode build settings
//
// A build setting can go further — into Info.plist via $(KEY) substitution in
// project.yml, and then to `Bundle.main` at runtime. Nothing does that today,
// because no value here is read by any code yet. The feature that first needs
// one adds that wiring itself.
//
// WHY THE `?` IN `#include?` MATTERS
// It means "include this file if it exists, and carry on quietly if it does
// not". That one character is what lets a fresh clone build with no setup at
// all: there are no secrets to supply yet, so there is nothing to configure.
// A plain `#include` would fail the build on a missing file and force every
// newcomer through a setup step before they could compile a skeleton.
//
// ORDER MATTERS. In an .xcconfig the last assignment to a key wins, so the
// include sits at the BOTTOM of this file. Everything above it is a default;
// anything in Secrets.xcconfig overrides it.
//
// WHAT MAY GO IN THIS FILE
// Only values that are safe to read in a public repository. Every real
// credential belongs in Secrets.xcconfig, which is git-ignored. See
// Config/Secrets.example.xcconfig for the keys and what each one is for.

// This file deliberately declares only what the app being built today needs.
// Keys for features that do not exist yet are NOT declared here — a committed
// default for an unbuilt feature is preparing for it, which this project's
// rules forbid. Config/Secrets.example.xcconfig documents what will be needed
// and when.

// --- Signing -----------------------------------------------------------------
// Your Apple Developer team, needed only to install on a physical iPhone.
// Simulator builds are never signed, so this is empty by default and a clone
// with no Apple developer account still builds and tests.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// --- Local overrides ---------------------------------------------------------
// Must stay last. See the note on ordering above.
#include? "Secrets.xcconfig"
